\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1}{\ignorespaces Technology architecture showing how chosen technologies map to system components across four layers. Arrows indicate data flow direction.}}{17}{figure.caption.13}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {2}{\ignorespaces Project Gantt Chart (weeks 1 to 13, January to April 2026)}}{19}{figure.caption.15}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3}{\ignorespaces Kanban board showing task progress across four stages: To Do, In Progress, Testing, and Done.}}{24}{figure.caption.21}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4}{\ignorespaces System Architecture showing the three-stage core pipeline. Data Retrieval is a stretch goal.}}{29}{figure.caption.23}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {5}{\ignorespaces Database entity-relationship diagram. Primary keys in bold, foreign keys in italics. All tables use UUID primary keys with row-level security. The dashed line indicates an optional foreign key (pitch books may or may not reference a template).}}{31}{figure.5}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {6}{\ignorespaces Swagger UI showing the REST API endpoints grouped by resource: Companies (data retrieval), Pitch Books (generation and CRUD), and Templates (upload and analysis).}}{33}{figure.caption.26}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {7}{\ignorespaces Wireframe: Dashboard. Sidebar navigation with four sections; main area shows aggregate stats and recent pitch books as cards.}}{34}{figure.caption.27}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {8}{\ignorespaces Wireframe: New Pitch Book form. Company name, ticker, PB type, optional template upload, and a free-text context field. The Generate button triggers the backend pipeline.}}{34}{figure.caption.28}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {9}{\ignorespaces Wireframe: Pitch Book Viewer (v1, web-only). No in-browser preview or editor, just a download link. This screen made the editing friction visible: every change required a round trip through PowerPoint, which led to the add-in design.}}{35}{figure.caption.29}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {10}{\ignorespaces Data flow through the generation pipeline. All template data passes through the Template Schema; there is no direct link from extraction to planning.}}{37}{figure.caption.31}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {11}{\ignorespaces Web app dashboard showing pitch book counts, recent generation history, and sidebar navigation.}}{39}{figure.caption.33}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {12}{\ignorespaces Orchestration pipeline. Each stage passes its output to the next. Dashed paths show graceful failure handling: data retrieval passes an empty object on API failure, and the content planner falls back to a generic plan if Zod validation fails.}}{39}{figure.caption.34}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {13}{\ignorespaces New pitch book creation form. The user selects a deck layout, colour theme, and enters company details before generating.}}{40}{figure.caption.35}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {14}{\ignorespaces Slide master view showing the available layouts for a template. Each layout defines placeholder positions, fonts, and background styling that the generation pipeline inherits.}}{43}{figure.caption.36}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {15}{\ignorespaces Original template with 14 slides across different layout types, each with a consistent brown and gold visual style.}}{46}{figure.caption.37}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {16}{\ignorespaces Generated output for Meta Platforms using the template from Figure~\ref {fig:template-original}. The 11 slides inherit the template's Felix Titling font, brown background, and gold accents. The add-in taskpane (right) shows slides were inserted directly into PowerPoint.}}{46}{figure.caption.38}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {17}{\ignorespaces System architecture with PowerPoint Add-in. The add-in and web app share the same backend and database. A pitch book generated in either interface is stored centrally and accessible from both.}}{48}{figure.caption.39}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {18}{\ignorespaces PowerPoint with the add-in taskpane open. Generated slides appear in the slide panel on the left, and the AI chat input sits at the bottom of the taskpane.}}{49}{figure.caption.40}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {19}{\ignorespaces Pitch book viewer showing slide preview, thumbnail navigation, and AI chat panel.}}{50}{figure.caption.41}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {20}{\ignorespaces Vitest UI showing all 14 test files and 134 tests passing across the three apps. The right panel displays the selected test's source code and can switch to console output on failure.}}{52}{figure.caption.43}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {21}{\ignorespaces Cypress test runner showing the E2E spec files grouped by feature area.}}{53}{figure.caption.45}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {22}{\ignorespaces A passing Cypress test. The left panel shows each automated step (clicking elements, checking text), and the right panel shows the browser state at each step.}}{53}{figure.caption.46}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {23}{\ignorespaces A failing Cypress test. The assertion expected either pitch book cards or an empty state message, but the data had not loaded yet when the check ran. This was a test logic error, not an application bug.}}{54}{figure.caption.47}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {24}{\ignorespaces GitHub Actions CI pipeline running lint, type checks, unit tests, E2E tests, and build on every push. All stages must pass before code can be merged to main.}}{55}{figure.caption.49}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {25}{\ignorespaces Vercel production deployment of the Next.js web app, deployed from the \texttt {main} branch.}}{55}{figure.caption.50}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {26}{\ignorespaces Render dashboard showing the Express backend service running with live logs.}}{56}{figure.caption.51}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {27}{\ignorespaces Generation time per evaluation run. The dashed line marks the mean (47.9s). Run 7 is the outlier at 78s due to repeated validation failures.}}{58}{figure.caption.55}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {28}{\ignorespaces Usability evaluation median scores (n=4). The add-in intuitiveness scored lowest. Time savings and reduced editing scored highest.}}{61}{figure.caption.60}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {29}{\ignorespaces Login page with email and password form alongside application branding.}}{82}{figure.caption.98}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {30}{\ignorespaces Registration page with name, email, and password fields.}}{82}{figure.caption.99}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {31}{\ignorespaces Forgot password page. Sends a reset link via Supabase Auth.}}{83}{figure.caption.100}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {32}{\ignorespaces Dashboard with summary cards, recent pitch books, and generation status indicators.}}{84}{figure.caption.102}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {33}{\ignorespaces Pitch books list with company name, type, date, and generation status. Includes search filtering.}}{85}{figure.caption.104}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {34}{\ignorespaces Pitch book viewer with full-size slide, thumbnail strip, and AI chat panel for requesting edits.}}{85}{figure.caption.105}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {35}{\ignorespaces New pitch book form with company details, pitch book type, transaction context, and design options.}}{86}{figure.caption.107}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {36}{\ignorespaces Templates page (empty state) with upload prompt for .pptx reference templates.}}{87}{figure.caption.109}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {37}{\ignorespaces Deck layouts overview with seven pre-configured deck types, each showing a description and slide count.}}{88}{figure.caption.111}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {38}{\ignorespaces Deck layout editor for Company Overview. Each slide has a title, layout type, and content description. Slides can be reordered, added, or deleted.}}{88}{figure.caption.112}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {39}{\ignorespaces Settings page with profile details, password change, and sign-out.}}{89}{figure.caption.114}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {40}{\ignorespaces Add-in after generation. 11 slides have been inserted into the presentation. The taskpane shows a completion message and an AI chat input for requesting edits.}}{90}{figure.caption.116}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {41}{\ignorespaces New pitch book form in the add-in. The user enters company name, ticker, pitch book type, transaction type, colour theme, design style, and optional context before generating.}}{91}{figure.caption.117}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {42}{\ignorespaces Generation in progress. The taskpane shows a step-by-step progress indicator: analysing template, fetching company data, planning content with AI, building slides, and generating previews.}}{92}{figure.caption.118}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {43}{\ignorespaces Completed generation for Apple Inc. The teal and coral colour theme was applied automatically. Slides appear in the left panel and the AI chat is ready for edit requests.}}{92}{figure.caption.119}%
